BCIs are no longer just lab concepts—they are tangible tools actively restoring function and independence to people with severe paralysis. Today, this technology primarily delivers on three major fronts: enabling communication, restoring movement, and providing innovative control methods with varying levels of invasiveness.

🗣️ Restoring Communication and Connection
For individuals who have lost the ability to speak due to conditions like ALS or brainstem stroke, BCIs are offering a new voice.

Achieving High-Speed, Accurate Speech: In a landmark study published in Nature Medicine, a participant named Casey Harrell used an intracortical BCI for nearly two years to independently communicate at home. The system decoded his neural signals into text, allowing him to "speak" at an average speed of 56 words per minute with over 99% word accuracy in testing. Over the study period, he communicated more than 183,000 sentences and nearly 2 million words.

Speaking in One's Own Voice: A major breakthrough from Neuralink has enabled an ALS patient named Kenneth Shock to do more than just convert thoughts to text. His implant decodes brain signals into phonemes (the smallest units of sound) which are then assembled into words and spoken aloud. Remarkably, the system was able to replicate his natural speaking voice from before his diagnosis, adding a profound layer of personal identity to the communication.

A New Way to Live: Neuralink patient Jon Noble described his 100 days with the implant as "giving me a new way to live," emphasizing how the technology restored his freedom and independence.

🦾 Regaining Movement and Independence
BCIs are also translating thoughts of movement into real-world actions, helping individuals interact with their environment.

Restoring Grasp Function: In July 2026, a patient in Shanghai received the world's first commercial BCI implant—the Neuracle NEO system. The device captures signals from the brain's surface (epidurally) to help patients with spinal cord injuries regain hand-grasping function. This allows them to perform daily activities like picking up a cup, a feat that had been impossible for them for years.

Intuitive Computer and Device Control: Patients are using BCIs to interact with the digital world seamlessly. Beyond controlling a computer cursor, Neuralink patient Jon Noble used his implant to play the complex video game World of Warcraft entirely through thought.

A Less Invasive Path to Control: Synchron's Stentrode is delivered to the brain via the jugular vein in a procedure that takes about 20-30 minutes, avoiding the need for open-brain surgery. The stent-like device, equipped with 16 sensors, expands to line the wall of a blood vessel near the motor cortex, translating thoughts into commands to wirelessly control computers or native Apple devices like an iPad.

🔬 A Spectrum of Technologies for Different Needs
The capabilities of a BCI are closely tied to how it interfaces with the brain. Different technical approaches offer a trade-off between invasiveness and the richness of the signal.

Approach	Key Player(s)	Capability Example
Highly Invasive (Electrodes penetrate brain tissue)	Neuralink	Enables high-bandwidth tasks like complex video game control and high-speed speech decoding
Semi-Invasive (Device rests on brain's surface)	Neuracle (NEO)	Restores motor function, like controlling a robotic glove for daily grasping tasks, with a less invasive surgical implant
Minimally Invasive (Device delivered via blood vessels)	Synchron (Stentrode)	Allows for wireless control of computers and digital devices for daily online tasks, without the need for open-skull surgery
💡 The Future is Being Built Now
The landscape is evolving rapidly, with research pushing the boundaries of what's possible.

High-Bandwidth Portals: Researchers at Stanford, Columbia, and other universities are developing next-generation BCIs like the Biological Interface System to Cortex (BISC). This single-chip implant has a total volume of just 3 mm³ and integrates 65,536 electrodes, creating a high-bandwidth, wireless connection to external computers to potentially treat conditions like epilepsy and paralysis.

Towards Human-AI Collaboration: Experts view the "blending of learning between humans and AI" as the next phase for BCIs, aiming for more sophisticated, lifelike functions like controlling a robotic arm with fluid, natural movement.

In short, BCIs are currently doing the remarkable work of restoring speech, movement, and digital interaction to people who have lost these abilities. While they are not yet consumer devices, they are powerful, practical medical tools that are changing lives today.

